\begin_layout Standard
 This report evaluates four uncertainty quantification (UQ) methods for underwater acoustic Transmission Loss (TL) computed by the Bellhop ray-tracing model.
 The uncertain simulation inputs modelled as independent Gaussian random variables are the sonar carrier frequency,
 source depth (
\begin_inset Formula $z_{S}$
\end_inset

),
 and the sound velocity profile (SVP).
 At minimum,
 we estimate the first moments of the TL distribution at each pixel —
 the mean 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{E}[\mathrm{TL}]$
\end_inset

 and variance 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{Var}[\mathrm{TL}]$
\end_inset

 —
 which together bound the detection probability via the one-sided Chebyshev (Cantelli) inequality (Eq.
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:cantelli"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
 Three advanced methods —
 1st- and 2nd-order Delta,
 Polynomial Chaos Expansion (PCE),
 and a 3-parameter lognormal (LN3) fit —
 are compared against Monte Carlo (MC) ground truth across four propagation scenarios (shallow water,
 deep water,
 upslope,
 downslope) and three input distributions (Normal
\begin_inset space \thinspace{}
\end_inset

1%/5%/10%).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Key conclusions:

\series default
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Delta method
\series default
 (1st-order Taylor) is reliable for flat bathymetry and small uncertainty (
\begin_inset Formula $\leq$
\end_inset

1% of nominal value),
 but the Hessian (2nd-order Taylor) correction already exceeds 96% of total variance at 5% uncertainty,
 indicating the stochastic field is not amenable to linear approximation at large perturbations.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
PCE
\series default
 (fitted to existing MC samples) requires order 
\begin_inset Formula $K^{*}=5$
\end_inset

–
\begin_inset Formula $12$
\end_inset

 and shows moderate to strong convergence across all scenario/distribution combinations.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Full MC
\series default
 is necessary for slope bathymetry (
\begin_inset Formula $\geq$
\end_inset

5% perturbation) and for 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{S}$
\end_inset

 uncertainty in deep water with Normal distributions.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Practical recommendation:

\series default
 For sonar mission planning,
 use PCE-
\begin_inset Formula $K^{*}$
\end_inset

 (order 5–9) in flat-bottom scenarios and fall back to MC for slope environments or large source-depth uncertainty.
 
\end_layout

